  "query_preview": "/FRIDAY NIGHT THROUGH THURSDAY/...\nIssued at 101 PM CDT Thu Sep 17 2026\n\nHigher Rain Chances Late Saturday thru Monday:\n\nGlobal models depict a broad mid-level shortwave finally\ntranslating east through the Plains and into the Midwest\nSaturday into Sunday. The latest suite of guidance has continued\nits slower/later trend with the passage of the attendant \nsurface front through Missouri sometime late Saturday night into\nSunday. The increased lift and modest shear along this front \nwill aid in convective initiation likely in the form of loosely\norganized clusters. The best rain chances will be across central\nMissouri, with decreasing chances as you look south where drier\nair will be present. Due to the slow nature of the front, rain\nchances will persist through Monday afternoon, but all day\nwashouts are not expected. \n\nNBM Probabilistic Rainfall (Sat Night thru Mon):\n>0.10 inch: 50-70%\n>0.25 inch: 40-60%\n>1.00 inch: 10-30%\n\nHeat through at Least Saturday:\n\nThere is medium to high confidence in continued afternoon heat \nindex values reaching near 100 deg through at least Saturday,\nhence the Heat Advisory through 8 PM Saturday evening. If the\naforementioned cold front really slows down as it passes through\nMissouri, or if convection/cloud cover is not quite as\nwidespread as global models suggest, then an extension of the\nadvisory may be needed across southern Missouri where\ntemperatures will remain abnormally hot. Confidence is not high\nenough to make that decision at this point, however. \n\nCooler Conditions Early Next Week:\n\nThere is high confidence that cooler temperatures will arrive\nnext week. What is less certain is exactly how much cooler these\ntemperatures will end up being. Ensembles continue to disagree\nin just how far the upper-level trough digs and how much the\nridge retreats; indeed, NBM temperature interquartile spreads\nincrease to 7-9 degrees by Monday.",
